 ### No-Body-Saddle Midpoint Transformation
-A normalization technique for cubes with no body saddles, mapping the theoretical saddle point $m$ into the block via linear scaling and analyzing gradients to constrain topology [S6, S6]. It computes the theoretical midpoint $m = (-c/a - d/a, -b/a)$. If $m$ is outside the block, linear transformations are applied to scale the block so $m$ is inside. It assumes $T_{mmm} = 0$ and that gradients along midpoint lines are constant [S6, S6]. This variant is used to resolve topological ambiguities in the absence of body saddles by constraining the possible face saddle configurations [S6, S6].
